
<p>Aaron Zitner, reporting for the Rupert Murdoch-owned Wall Street Journal:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Rarely in American politics has a president traded away so much
political support so quickly.</p>

<p>The losses that Republicans suffered on Tuesday marked one of the
most decisive repudiations of a president in modern history.
President Trump had re-entered the White House with advantages he
lacked in his first term: a victory in the popular vote, a cadre
of aides eager to expand his authority and cultural currency that
had NFL players doing the “Trump dance” in the end zone.</p>

<p>Only two years later, his political standing has collapsed, all
because of a dramatic miscalculation of his power to shape events.
A war of choice with Iran and tariffs imposed on U.S. trading
partners betrayed his promise to avoid foreign entanglements and
tame inflation. His extraordinary interventions in his party’s
primaries left Republicans with a set of unusually weak
candidates.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>The Democrats went from out of power in Washington to a massive majority in the House and an apparent 55-45 or even 56-44 majority in the Senate. <a href="https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-elections/georgia-governor-results">Keisha Lance Bottoms was elected the first black woman governor</a> in history — in <em>Georgia</em>. By a 9-point margin. <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/04/us/texas-talarico-hinojosa-abbott-paxton.html?unlocked_article_code=1.N1E.-7W3.vGY-dcYSYFiL">Texas elected Democrats</a> to the Senate and governor’s mansion. Kansas elected a Democratic senator <a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/11/03/us/kansas-senate-marshall-hamilton.html?unlocked_article_code=1.N1E.6vJy.RMWLyOyKsCLa">for the first time in 94 years</a>.</p>

<p>The fever, finally, well and truly broke.</p>

